From: Jacob Cook <jacobmcook1984@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] Input: axp20x-pek - do not report the key press that woke the system

When the power key is the wakeup source, the press is delivered to
userspace after resume and systemd-logind suspends the system straight
back to sleep.

The AXP288 clears the pending PEK bits from its noirq resume callback,
but that only works for a real suspend-to-RAM where the IRQ thread
cannot run until the devices are back. Under s2idle the nested
regmap-irq thread runs as soon as the PMIC interrupt fires and calls
this handler while the system is still officially suspended, so
anything done at resume time is too late.

Drop the press while pm_suspend_in_progress(): the interrupt has already
done its job as a wakeup source. The release is still reported. The same
handler serves it, and the input core ignores an event that matches the
current key state, so swallowing it would leave KEY_POWER held down and
the next press would never reach userspace.

Seen on the Anbernic RG35XX H (H700 + AXP717): every power-key wake from
s2idle was followed by "Power key pressed short" / "Suspending..." from
systemd-logind in the same second.

---
--- a/drivers/input/misc/axp20x-pek.c
+++ b/drivers/input/misc/axp20x-pek.c
@@ -26,6 +26,7 @@
 #include <linux/platform_device.h>
 #include <linux/regmap.h>
 #include <linux/slab.h>
+#include <linux/suspend.h>
 
 #define AXP20X_PEK_STARTUP_MASK		(0xc0)
 #define AXP20X_PEK_SHUTDOWN_MASK	(0x03)
@@ -204,10 +205,19 @@
 	 * The power-button is connected to ground so a falling edge (dbf)
 	 * means it is pressed.
 	 */
-	if (irq == axp20x_pek->irq_dbf)
+	if (irq == axp20x_pek->irq_dbf) {
+		/*
+		 * A press that woke the system has done its job; reporting it
+		 * would suspend again at once. The release is still reported so
+		 * the key is not left held.
+		 */
+		if (pm_suspend_in_progress())
+			return IRQ_HANDLED;
+
 		input_report_key(idev, KEY_POWER, true);
-	else if (irq == axp20x_pek->irq_dbr)
+	} else if (irq == axp20x_pek->irq_dbr) {
 		input_report_key(idev, KEY_POWER, false);
+	}
 
 	input_sync(idev);
 
